  "script": "def solve(grid):\n    # Find 2x2 blocks of 2s and 2x2 blocks of 1s, and isolated 1s\n    rows, cols = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n    \n    twos = []\n    for r in range(rows-1):\n        for c in range(cols-1):\n            if grid[r][c]==grid[r][c+1]==grid[r+1][c]==grid[r+1][c+1]==2:\n                twos.append((r,c))\n    \n    ones_2x2 = []\n    for r in range(rows-1):\n        for c in range(cols-1):\n            if grid[r][c]==grid[r][c+1]==grid[r+1][c]==grid[r+1][c+1]==1:\n                ones_2x2.append((r,c))\n    \n    # Find isolated 1s (not part of any 2x2 block of 1s)\n    in_block = set()\n    for r,c in ones_2x2:\n        for dr in range(2):\n            for dc in range(2):\n                in_block.add((r+dr,c+dc))\n    \n    isolated = [(r,c) for r in range(rows) for c in range(cols) if grid[r][c]==1 and (r,c) not in in_block]\n    \n    # Each 2x2 block (of 2 or 1) and each isolated 1 is an item\n    # Pair 2x2-2 blocks with nearby 2x2-1 blocks, and check if isolated 1 is nearby\n    # Output has 5 elements, one per 2x2 block pair? \n    # Count: each example has 5 2x2 blocks total? No...\n    \n    # Let me count: Ex1 has 3 twos blocks, 3 ones blocks, 3 isolated ones\n    # Output length 5... \n    \n    # Maybe: for each 2x2 block of 2s, check if there's an isolated 1 adjacent/nearby -> output 1, else 0\n    # Plus for each 2x2 block of 1s without a paired 2 block -> output 1\n    \n    # Actually output is length 5 with some 1s and 0s. Let me think differently.\n    # \n    # 5 2x2 blocks total (twos + ones_2x2). For each, is there an isolated 1 nearby?\n    \n    result = []\n    all_blocks = [(r,c,'2') for r,c in twos] + [(r,c,'1') for r,c in ones_2x2]\n    \n    for br, bc, typ in all_blocks:\n        has_isolated = False\n        for ir, ic in isolated:\n            # Check if isolated 1 is adjacent to this block\n            block_cells = {(br+dr,bc+dc) for dr in range(2) for dc in range(2)}\n            for brc in block_cells:\n